<!--{ pr01 : rich }-->
# 12. Your first package

The last lesson of the core path: a folder of your own, for your own
documents. This lesson has no checks, since your package is outside the
tutorial. About five minutes.
<!--{ pr02 : rich }-->
## Make a package

A **package** is a folder of documents that belong together, such as the
calculations for one structure, with the references they use. Everything it
needs is inside its folder, so it works as a whole wherever you copy it: to
a colleague, to a server, into a backup.

In the second terminal, make one, for example in a folder `first-package`
of your home folder:

```
cd ~
stilo init first-package
```

`stilo init` creates the folder if needed, writes `package.texl`, which marks
it as a package, and copies the Stilo library into `references/stilo`, which
data tables need (lesson 7). On Windows, write the folder as you usually do, as
`stilo init C:\Users\you\first-package`.
<!--{ pr03 : rich }-->
## Serve it

`stilo serve` opens a folder in the editor, as `stilo tutor` opens this
tutorial. The tutorial already uses the port 8010, so give your package
another one:

```
stilo serve first-package --port 8011
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:8011/` in your browser, press **Alt+N** for a new
document, and write. Everything from the lessons works there. Press **Ctrl+C**
in the terminal to stop it.

Next time, stop the tutorial first and run `stilo serve first-package`,
which uses the port 8010.
<!--{ pr04 : rich }-->
## References

The folder `references` holds the **references** of the package: other
packages, or plain folders of documents, that its documents import, as a
report lists its references. In an import, `~` stands for the package
folder, so every document of the package imports the Stilo library as
`~/references/stilo/index.texl`, however deep in its folders it is. Copy the
folder `references/steel` of this tutorial into the `references` of your
package, and every document of it can import
`~/references/steel/sections.texl`, as in lesson 8.

## Serving to others

`stilo serve` has no accounts and no passwords: anybody who can reach it can
change every document of the folder. By default it accepts connections from
your own computer only. Keep it that way, unless you put your own access
control in front of it.

More in the manual: [Packages and references](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/editor/projects.html),
[stilo init](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/cli/init.html) and
[stilo serve](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/cli/serve.html).
<!--{ pr05 : rich }-->
